<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaoxing</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaoxing is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> Bay. As Kuaiji it was the centre of the ancient State of Yue, and nearby Mount Kuaiji is the traditional burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>. The city is known for its canals, its Shaoxing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> wine and as the birthplace of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaoxing.jpg" alt="Shaoxing" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zhangzhugang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaoxing_Bazi_Qiao_2017.12.09_16-16-06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shaoxing is a prefecture-level city on the southern shore of Hangzhou Bay in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> province, on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river">Qiantang River</a> estuary. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a> to the east, Taizhou to the south-east, Jinhua to the south-west and Hangzhou to the west. The city was first named Shaoxing in 1131 during the Southern Song dynasty, after the reign period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-song">Emperor Gaozong of Song</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city lies north of the Kuaiji Mountains, an important centre of the people of Yue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>. Chinese legend connected the mountains with events in the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>, the founder of the Xia. Around the early 5th century BCE, in the time of the Yue king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">Goujian</a>, his people began to establish permanent centres on the plain north of the hills. After his release from captivity in Wu, Goujian commissioned his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> to build a major triangular fortification in the area of the present Yuecheng District.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquest in 222 BCE the First Emperor visited Mount Kuaiji in the last year of his reign, 210 BCE, and sacrificed to the spirit of Yu. The stele he erected is lost, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> saw it and preserved its text in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>. The area's capital was known as Kuaiji until the 12th century, when it was renamed Shaoxing.</p><p>Under the Ming and Qing dynasties Shaoxing was a prefecture of eight counties. From the later Ming through the Qing it was known, sometimes with disapproval, for its network of natives in the imperial bureaucracy, who cooperated out of loyalty to their place of origin. It also supplied unofficial legal specialists, the Shaoxing masters, whose knowledge of the Qing legal code let officials trained in the Confucian Classics judge local cases. In the early 20th century the prefecture was abolished and its name applied to a new county.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shaoxing is a prefecture-level city administering three districts, two county-level cities and one county. It is known for Shaoxing rice wine, brewed with the water of Mirror Lake, and for Yue opera, sung in the local dialect. Its sites include the Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>, the Orchid Pavilion, commemorating the calligrapher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi">Wang Xizhi</a>, and the Shen Garden, associated with the poet Lu You. Several places connected with the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>, who was born in the city, survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaoxing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eastern Zhejiang Canal began as the Shanyin Canal, excavated in <b>Shaoxing</b> by the Yue official Fan Li in the early 5th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Yue, or Yuyue, was a state of the Yue peoples around <b>Kuaiji</b> (Shaoxing) in northern Zhejiang.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-yue">State of Yue</a></li></ul></section>
